===Verb===
{{en-verb|++}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|politics}} To [[disseminate]] (something as) [[political]] [[propaganda]], especially [[communist]] propaganda, through [[art]], [[drama]], [[literature]], etc.
#* {{quote-book|en|editors=[[w:Harry W. Laidler|Harry W[ellington] Laidler]]; w:Norman Thomas|title=The Socialism of Our Times: A Symposium|series=Prelude to Depression|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Da Capo Press|year=1929|year_published=1976|page=241|isbn=978-0-306-70850-3|passage=Anyone who has seen a class struggler at work, '''agitpropping''' around Union Square, or the back streets of the Loop, realizes that, by the very nature of things, he or she is so possessed of that particular barricading dream as to lose touch with all reality, [...]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=Trace|location=London|publisher=Villiers Publications|year=1957|page=10|issn=0496-005X|oclc=456040221|passage=With respect to a poet's facility in logomachy, boredom in the area of power politics theorisation, the seriousness or gaiety with which he '''agitprops''' his ego, the degree of his attitude toward the superior poet, or the rebelliousness or conformity of his disciples?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|editor=[[w:István Kertész|Stephen D[enis] Kertesz]]|title=The Task of Universities in a Changing World|series=International Studies of the Committee on International Relations, University of Notre Dame|location=Notre Dame, Ind.|publisher=w:University of Notre Dame Press|year=1971|page=89|isbn=978-0-268-00444-6|passage=This is to suggest that the university will leave behind its more limited work and bourgeois integrity in order to undertake the task of brain-trusting and '''agitpropping''' for the revolution.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Fatma Mansur|title=Phoenix in Her Blood: A Historical Entertainment|location=Beverley, North Humberside|publisher=Eothen Press|year=1987|page=157|isbn=978-0-906719-10-7|passage=Envoys and missionaries '''agitpropped''' in the four corners of the Empire, [...]}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Mel Gussow|title=Review/Theater; apartheid re-echoes in a musical rendering of a strike’s bitterness|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150525200719/https://www.nytimes.com/1990/12/20/theater/review-theater-apartheid-re-echoes-musical-rendering-strike-s-bitterness.html|archivedate=25 May 2015|newspaper=w:The New York Times|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:The New York Times Company|date=20 December 1990|section=C|page=11|column=1|issn=0362-4331|oclc=971436363|passage=More an insecure platform for Mr. [[w:Mbongeni Ngema|[Mbongeni] Ngema]]'s didacticism than a play with a life of its own, "Township Fever" is '''agit-propped''' with polemical sloganeering.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=w:Instauration|location=Cape Canaveral, Fla.|publisher=Howard Allen Enterprises|year=1993|page=31|column=1|issn=0277-2302|oclc=6406234|passage=The double agent was {{w|Grant Bristow}}, who '''agit-propped''' for—not against—the right-wing Heritage Front and is now hiding in a safe house.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Richard Grossinger|chapter=|title=On the Integration of Nature: Post-9/11 Biopolitical Notes|location=Berkeley, Calif.|publisher=w:North Atlantic Books|year=2005|page=99|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=AIfkTm5gbcUC&pg=PA99|isbn=978-1-55643-603-1|passage=I knew that he [Allen Van Newkirk] '''agitpropped''' other poetry readings and media events, e.g. the Underground Press Syndicate conference in Madison (shouting, "All media are lies").}}

